To terminate a C program, you can use the exit() function from the stdlib.h library. The syntax for using this function is as follows:

#include <stdlib.h>

int main()
{
    // Your code here...

    exit(0);  // Terminate the program with an exit status of 0 (success)

    return 0;
}

The exit() function takes one argument: the exit status of the program. A value of 0 typically indicates success, while any other value may indicate an error or abnormal termination.

Note that calling exit() will cause your program to immediately stop executing and return control to the operating system
